-- title:
--   Node kernels of a Cartier quadruple are coordinate lines
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota \colon W(\mathbb F_{p^2}) \to W(k)$ (here `Zp2 p` is $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$). Write $\bar\iota$ for $\iota$ followed by the quotient map $W(k) \to W(k)/(p)$, where `pIdeal p B` denotes the ideal generated by $p$.
--
--   The first group of hypotheses concerns a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $W(k)/(p)$, i.e. a two-dimensional commutative formal group law with an action of $W(\mathbb F_{p^2})$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ satisfying $\varpi \circ \varpi = [p]$ and $\varpi \circ [a] = [\sigma a] \circ \varpi$. It is assumed that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\iota$ (`hΦ`: the weight-zero and weight-one parts of the Lie module are complementary and both invertible over $W(k)/(p)$), that $\Phi$ has height $4$ (`hΦ4`: the series $[p]$ has kernel of degree $p^4$), that the weight-zero Lie part is annihilated by `lieVarpi`, the linear part of $\varpi$ on the Lie module (`h0Φ`), and that the graded pieces of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ in degrees $0$ and $1$ for $\bar\iota$ are complementary (`hcΦ`), the degree-$n$ piece consisting of those $f$ with $[\tau(c)]\cdot f = \bar\iota(\tau(c))^{p^n} f$ for all $c \in \mathbb F_{p^2}$, where $\tau$ is the Teichmüller lift. A rigidification is fixed: an additive map $r_\Phi \colon \mathbb Z_p^2 \to \mathrm{NMod}$ of the graded Cartier module data attached to $\Phi$, $\bar\iota$ and `hcΦ`, subject to `hrΦ`, which requires that for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on that data, $r_\Phi$ maps the whole of $\mathbb Z_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-zero eta piece $\mathrm{eta}(L) \cap \mathrm{nPiece}\,0$.
--
--   The second group concerns the edge family. An element $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Q_p)$ is fixed with $g = \mathrm{diag}(p,1)$ (`hg`). Over the edge ring $E =$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p (W(k)/(p))` a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X$ is given together with $\gamma \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathrm{Cart}(X)$ such that: `hγ` states that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map $\iota$ followed by $W(k)/(p) \to E$, that is, $\gamma_i$ lies in the degree-$i$ graded piece and the matrix of tangent vectors $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_k)$ has unit determinant; `hγa` states that $\gamma$ has the edge structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p (W(k)/(p))`, i.e. for each $i$ and each $N$ the element $\varpi(\gamma_i)$ agrees, modulo $V^N$, with $\sum_{m<N} V^m\bigl([a_{m,i}]\gamma_{\pi(m,i)}\bigr)$; `hXs` states that $X$ is special for the same structure map, and `hX4` that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   The third group specialises to the node. A ring homomorphism $f_0 \colon E \to W(k)/(p)$ is given which splits the structure map ($f_0 \circ \mathrm{algebraMap} = \mathrm{id}$, `hf₀`) and kills both chart parameters, $f_0(\xi) = 0$ and $f_0(\eta) = 0$ (`hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η`). A natural number $m$ and a series $\rho_0$ over $W(k)/(p)$ are given with `hρ₀`: $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi \to X \otimes_{f_0} W(k)/(p)$ of height $4m$, i.e. an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Let $t_0$ be the rigidified triple consisting of the base change $X \otimes_{f_0} W(k)/(p)$, the integer $m$, and the reduction of $\rho_0$ modulo $p$. Then: `hOD₀` asserts that the reduced series of $t_0$ is an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism from the reduction of $\Phi$ to the reduction of the underlying module of $t_0$; `hcb` asserts the complementarity of the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces of $t_0$ after base change along the identity of $W(k)/(p)$, and `hcΦg` the same complementarity on the $\Phi$-side.
--
--   The fourth hypothesis is the normalisation `hN`, governed by a natural number $a$: for each $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$,
--   $$p^a \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(e_i) = p^{a+m} \cdot v_i,$$
--   where $\mathrm{rigidNum}$ is the additive map $\mathbb Z_p^2 \to \mathrm{NMod}$ of the graded data of $t_0$ obtained by composing $r_\Phi$ with the $N$-module maps induced by the base change on $\Phi$ and by the reduced isogeny of $t_0$, $e_i = \mathrm{Pi.single}\ i\ 1$, and $v_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_0,0)$, $v_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}\bigl(\mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_1,0)\bigr)$, with $\bar\gamma_i$ the image of $\gamma_i$ on Cartier modules under base change along $f_0$, then along the quotient by $(p)$, then along the reduction of the identity.
--
--   Finally, let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb Z_p$-algebra, let $y_0 \colon W(k)/(p) \to \Omega$ be a ring homomorphism, and let $Q$ be a Drinfeld datum over $\Omega$ for the uniformiser $p \in \mathbb Z_p$: full $\mathbb Z_p$-lattices $N_0(x) \le N_1(x)$ in $\mathbb Q_p^2$ for each $x \in \operatorname{Spec}\Omega$, depending openly on $x$ and with $p N_1(x) \le N_0(x)$, invertible $\Omega$-modules $T_0, T_1$ with maps $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ composing to multiplication by $p$ in either order, and, for each $x$, $\mathrm{locRing}\,\Omega\,x$-linear maps $u_0(x), u_1(x)$ from the base-changed lattices to the stalks of $T_0, T_1$ at $x$, compatible with inclusion and with multiplication by $p$. The hypothesis `hQ` asserts that the base change of $t_0$ along $y_0$, together with $Q$, satisfies `IsCartierQuadruple` for $\iota$, `hcΦ`, $r_\Phi$ and the structure map $y_0 \circ (\text{quotient by } (p))$: the reduced series is an $\mathcal O_D$-homomorphism, there are isomorphisms of $T_0$ and $T_1$ with the weight-zero and weight-one Lie parts intertwining $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ with `lieVarpi`, and for every prime $x$ the lattices $N_0(x)$ and $N_1(x)$ consist exactly of the vectors carried by eta sections of degree $0$, respectively $1$, over some localisation away from $x$, with further clauses computing $u_0(x)$ and $u_1(x)$ on such vectors in terms of the associated classes (these clauses are summarised here).
--
--   Fix a prime $x$ of $\Omega$ and assume the two lattice identities: `h₀` states $N_0(x) = \mathbb Z_p^2$, the standard full lattice in $\mathbb Q_p^2$, and `h₁` states that $N_1(x)$ is obtained from the standard full lattice by acting first with $g$ and then with the scalar matrix $p^{-1}$, the unit of $\mathbb Q_p$ attached to $p \ne 0$ in $\mathbb Z_p$; thus $N_1(x) = p^{-1} g\,\mathbb Z_p^2$.
--
--   The conclusion is a conjunction of two kernel computations over the local ring $\mathrm{locRing}\,\Omega\,x = \Omega_x$.
--
--   First, $\ker u_0(x)$ is the $\Omega_x$-submodule spanned by the single element obtained by transporting, along the equality `h₀.symm` of lattices via `transportEquiv`, the element $0 \otimes e_0 + 1 \otimes e_1$ of $\Omega_x \otimes_{\mathbb Z_p} \mathbb Z_p^2$, where $e_0, e_1$ are the standard basis vectors of the standard full lattice.
--
--   Second, $\ker u_1(x)$ is the $\Omega_x$-submodule spanned by the single element obtained as follows: take $1 \otimes e_0 + 0 \otimes e_1$ in $\Omega_x \otimes_{\mathbb Z_p} \mathbb Z_p^2$, transport it by the base-changed action of $g$, then by the base-changed action of the scalar matrix $p^{-1}$, and finally transport the result along the equality `h₁.symm` into the base change of $N_1(x)$.
--
--   This is the kernel half of the identification, in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation, of the Cartier quadruple attached to the normalised node triple with the Drinfeld quadruple of a node: under the two lattice identities $N_0(x) = \mathbb Z_p^2$ and $N_1(x) = p^{-1}g\,\mathbb Z_p^2$, the kernels of the two stalk maps are the coordinate lines singled out by the standard edge chart. It is combined with the lattice half in [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.lattice_eq_and_ker_eq_span_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.lattice_eq_and_ker_eq_span_of_isCartierQuadruple_map_node_of_rigidNum_single_eq), which feeds the recognition of node points of Drinfeld quadruples.
